A||N||13||Monkeyman||Am I getting ripped???||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||03-03-2000||08:01 PM||||Supposedly, my GT needs a new crank and bearings.  I spoke with the only mechanic in town that will work on Fieros today and this is what he quoted me:<P>$885US if I bring the car to him and he drops the cradle, performs the work (replace crank and bearings, etc), reinstalls the motor, and refills the fluids.<P>or<P>$275US if I bring him the motor only and he does the work.<P>I figure it will cost me about $30 in fluids, etc.  I don't have $885.  Not even close, but I need to get the car running soon.  Is it really worth $610 for someone to drop the cradle and reinstall everything?  Seems like alot to me.<P>Does the $275 seem reasonable???<P>Someone let me know before I get bent over!||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||03-03-2000||08:13 PM||||There is a lot of labor (time) involved in dropping a cradle. That's why a lot of us try to do it ourselves.<P>But, I'd be more concerned about that mechanic quoting you prices to repair an engine that he doesn't have a clue what is really wrong yet. I bet he won't warranty his work. Chances are that it needs a complete rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TwisT||03-03-2000||08:36 PM||||Monkeyman,  I work at a Honda/ Yamaha dealership here in Yankton South Dakota,  and I get to see first hand what's involved with repair costs.  That $800+ figure should be reasonable,  considering the amount of labor that's involved with exuming the engine out of a Fiero.  That's the part where you "bite the big one" on cost;  LABOR.  Sucks bigtime,  even our motorcycle shop has a labor flat-rate of $39 an hour...<P>Save a bundle by pulling the motor,  spend a bundle by leaving it in.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||03-03-2000||11:12 PM||||Monkey ... I've got a better idea!<P>Spend a few bucks, get the car down to my house, give william_thorniii, BENGAL4 and fiero56 a call, and the five of us will knock it out in a weekend!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000004||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||05:18 AM||||Scott--I like your suggestion, but once we get the motor out, how am I gonna get it back to the house to get it fixed?  Then I have to get it back down there to get put back in.  Now if I just had enough $$, I'd buy a complete rebuild kit and we could just rebuild it once we had it out!  It's all about money (or lack of it!).  I may just have to wait until I can afford to get the motor rebuilt.  I think the shop quoted me somewhere around $1700 for the rebuild if they removed the motor.  I'm not having any luck finding a decent motor that I can afford.  (Of course, I can't afford much right now.  I was gonna have to scramble to come up with the $275.)  I hate to say this, but I may have to buy a $200 Yugo to drive while I save up the $$ for another motor. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||05:20 AM||||batboy--I just told the mechanic that it needed a new crank and bearings (that's what the seller said--I don't have any idea), so that's the work that was quoted.  What makes you think that it might need a complete rebuild?  (It has around 100k miles on it.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||William Federle||03-04-2000||06:24 AM||||Monkeyman, I think I read you twice say you didn't know where to find a decent V6 to put in your new car so I hope you won't be offended if I make a suggestion. I know everyone's not crazy about V8 Archie but a while back he posted a letter on his list that said he was using v6's out of conversions as weights to hold the rear end of his truck down in the snow. The point is he probably has quite a few laying around and might even have a good idea of their condition (assuming they were driven in). It's just a thought - he's not to far away either.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||06:39 AM||||I couldn't be <B>less</B> offended!  Thanx for the suggestion.  I hadn't thought about Archie.  I'll email him right now.  Maybe he'd like to donate one to "the cause"? hehehe||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||03-04-2000||09:20 AM||||Bob Steiger at Twin Lakes Fiero in Illinois may have some reasonable engines around.<P>He's not that far from Indiana..<P>Phone 217-379-9140<P>Or you could get one of his 3.4 crate engines (brand new) for $4-5 K fully installed.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 03-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||turbo86se||03-04-2000||10:15 AM||||Hey monkeyman, in case you need to buy an engine rebuild kit, a couple weeks ago I saw a complete 3.2 rebuild kit on ebay, starting bid at $700. Last time I checked it out no one had bid on it. This guy is located in Texas and does lots of 2.8 to 3.2 rebuilds. This is the same guy with the red Indy for sale. <BR>I wish I could help you out with your engine search!  I'll keep my eyes open!||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||03-04-2000||10:15 AM||||I can find wrecked and rusted out cars all the time that still have a running engine. I know where a 87 Beretta is right now with a running 2.8 and auto. The kid slid into a curb on a snowy day and bent up the control arm and knocked the strut out of whack. Didn't do the wheel much good either. I can get these cars for about $300 to $500. I found a $165 rusted out 85 Olds with a good running 2.5, I didn't buy it because I need a 87 2.5 (DIS engine). Sometimes you can part out the car and make most of your money back out of it and still keep the engine and other stuff you need. Look around, ask around. I've had wrecked and rusted out cars given to me before.||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||03-04-2000||10:22 AM||||Monkey, about your engine needing rebuilt, if it needs a new crank and bearings, then it'll probably need rods too. You have to pull the pistons out to get to the rods. Oh my, look at the cylinder wall scoring. Better bore the block and get new pistons. Gotta get a complete gasket set anyway. Might as well put in a new oil pump and cam and lifters. The timing chain's looking worn, need a new timing gear and chain set. The valves need ground, send the heads into the shop to be rebuilt. Hmmmm, guess the whole engine needed rebuilt after all. Trust me, been there and done that. I'm sure many other here have too. Cheapest route is to find a good running low-mileage engine with good compression and good oil pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||01:47 PM||||Thanx, guys.  If anyone finds a decent running 2.8 for cheap, please let me know.  If I had the $$ for a rebuild kit, I'd probably go that way, but I just don't have the cash right now.  It's a catch 22.  I need a good running car so I can get a job, but I need a job so I can pay to get the car running!  Anybody want to loan me $1500?  (Just kidding!)||||reg||1
Z||000013||RFMike||03-06-2000||11:27 PM||||MM,<BR>I agree with Batboy.  If the crank and bearings are shot, you're looking at more work.  <BR>My advice is to buy a rebuilt engine.  You can get one for just under a grand, with a 12 month/12k warranty from several small remanufacturers.  You still have to put it in, but you would be looking at about the same price of the rebuild if the shop pulled the old one and installed the new one.  <P>If you could scrape the cash together, and could do the removal and installation yourself, you could buy a reman from Jasper.  I've spent some time looking into remans, and they may have the best.  They've won Best Remanufacturer three times in the past ten years (national selection, have a racing background, offer a 3 year/ 75,000 mile warranty, and for $1,754, they deliver a motor with remanufactured block, heads, cam, crank, and rods.  The standard set of new internal parts includes: pistons, rings, bearings, lifters, valves, valve springs, and timing components.  Included with the engine will be the oil pump, oil pan, and the timing cover.  These are already installed on the engine for you. That price includes the 3.2 crank.  <BR>I've had some tight times myself, so I feel for you.  Hope you can make something work for you.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
